{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.CoordinationBulkCriterion","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Deriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Asymptotics","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.affine_over_affine_not_surfaceTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.affine_over_affine_not_surfaceTerm","kind":"theorem","line":287,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L287-L307","doc":"**An affine singular count over an affine cell count is not a surface term.** Their ratio tends\nto the nonzero constant `α_s/α_c` — a constant renormalisation of the bulk, exactly as the\nhyperbolic ball gives. Every Seifert invariant of the canonical closure measured so far is affine\nin `A`; whether the singular count is too is the open computation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.StokesSurfaceTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.StokesSurfaceTerm","kind":"def","line":280,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L280-L285","doc":"Yekutieli's estimate splits into a bulk term counted by the `4^k` kites (an area count) and\na boundary term counted by the singular locus (one dimension down). Read as functions of the mass\nnumber, the singular count is a **genuine surface term** iff it grows without bound while vanishing\nas a fraction of the kite count — the same conjunction as `HasBulk`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hasDerivAt_hypVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hasDerivAt_hypVol","kind":"theorem","line":266,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L266-L276","doc":"**`V'(R) = S(R)`**: the area follows from Bolyai's volume (held, `horvath2010` §3.1) by\ndifferentiation, so it rests on the held source too. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hyperbolic_perNucleon_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hyperbolic_perNucleon_le","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L251-L262","doc":"**The hyperbolic \"surface term\" is a constant shift of the volume coefficient, not a decaying\ncorrection.** With `k' < k` the per-nucleon binding sits at least `ε(k − k')` below the\nuniform-degree plateau at every `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hyperbolic_not_hasBulk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hyperbolic_not_hasBulk","kind":"theorem","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L242-L249","doc":"**A hyperbolic-ball cluster has no bulk, under ANY positive radius assignment.** Its surface\nfraction is `≥ 2` at every `A`, so it never vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypSurfaceCount_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypSurfaceCount_div","kind":"theorem","line":237,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L237-L240","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypSurfaceCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypSurfaceCount","kind":"def","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L232-L235","doc":"The surface count of a hyperbolic-ball cluster under a radius assignment `Rof`: nucleon count\n`A` extensive in volume, surface count extensive in area, so `A_surf = (S/V)(R(A)) · A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.not_hasBulk_of_fraction_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.not_hasBulk_of_fraction_ge","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L224-L230","doc":"**A surface fraction bounded below by a positive constant refutes `HasBulk`.** Generic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucFraction_tendsto_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucFraction_tendsto_zero","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L217-L220","doc":"Euclidean: the surface fraction **vanishes** — the origin of every `A^(−1/3)` surface term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucFraction_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucFraction_eq","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L211-L215","doc":"Euclidean: `S/V = 3/R`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucArea","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucArea","kind":"def","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L209-L209","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.eucVol","kind":"def","line":208,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L208-L208","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.surfaceFraction_sub_two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.surfaceFraction_sub_two_le","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L187-L204","doc":"The residual `A`-dependence: `S/V − 2 ≤ 4R/(sinh 2R − 2R)`, which decays like `R e^(−2R)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.surfaceFraction_ge_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.surfaceFraction_ge_two","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L181-L185","doc":"**The hyperbolic surface fraction is at least 2, for every radius.** In Euclidean space it is\n`3/R → 0`; here it never drops below a constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.two_mul_hypVol_le_hypArea","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.two_mul_hypVol_le_hypArea","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L173-L179","doc":"**`2V ≤ S` for every `R`** — the whole content is `e^(−x) ≥ 1 − x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea_sub_two_mul_hypVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea_sub_two_mul_hypVol","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L163-L171","doc":"**`S − 2V = 2π (e^(−2R) + 2R − 1)`**, exactly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypVol_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypVol_pos","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L156-L161","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea_eq","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L151-L154","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypArea","kind":"def","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L148-L149","doc":"Area of the boundary sphere of that ball: `4π sinh² R`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.hypVol","kind":"def","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HyperbolicSurfaceLaw.lean#L145-L146","doc":"Volume of a ball of radius `R` in `H³` (curvature `−1`): `π (sinh 2R − 2R)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}